1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,360
I have had the distinct blessing in my life to have worked on a bunch of amazing projects,

2
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:25,160
but the coolest project I ever worked on was around this guy.

3
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:26,720
This guy's name is Tempt.

4
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:31,320
Tempt was one of the foremost graffiti artists in the 80s, and he came home from a run one

5
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:35,940
day and said, dad, my legs are tingling, and that was the onset of ALS.

6
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:38,040
So Tempt is now completely paralyzed.

7
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:40,800
He only has use of his eyes.

8
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:45,000
I was exposed to him and my company, I have a company that does design and animation,

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,760
so obviously graffiti is definitely an intricate part of what we admire and respect in the

10
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:50,600
art world.

11
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:54,320
So we decided that we were going to sponsor Tony and Tempt and his cause.

12
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:59,720
So I went and met with his brother and father and said, we're going to give you this money,

13
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:00,720
what are you going to do with it?

14
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:04,240
And his brother said, I just want to be able to talk to Tony again.

15
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:06,960
I just want to be able to communicate with him and him to be able to communicate with

16
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:07,960
me.

17
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:10,640
And I said, well, wait a second, isn't that, I mean, I've seen Stephen Hawking, don't all

18
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:14,520
paralyzed people have that ability to communicate via these devices?

19
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:20,000
And he said, no, unless you're in the upper echelon and you've got really amazing insurance,

20
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,440
you can't actually do that, that these devices aren't accessible to people.

21
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:24,720
I said, well, how do you actually communicate?

22
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:28,600
And he said, we run it, did everyone see the movie, The Diving Bell and the Butterfly?

23
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:29,680
That's how they communicate.

24
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:34,200
So run their finger along, that's archaic, how can that be?

25
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:40,880
So I showed up with the desire to just write a check and instead I wrote a check that I

26
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:43,680
had no freaking idea how I was going to cash.

27
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:47,160
I committed to his brother and his father right then and there, I'm like, all right,

28
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:48,160
here's the deal.

29
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:50,960
Tony's going to speak, we're going to get him a machine and we're going to figure out

30
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:55,200
a way for him to do his art again, because it's a travesty that someone who still has

31
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,060
all of that in them isn't able to communicate it.

32
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:02,600
So I spoke at a conference a couple months after that, I met these guys called GRL, Graffiti

33
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:07,960
Research Lab, and they have a technology that allows them to project the light on any surface

34
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:11,960
and then with a laser pointer draw on it and it just registers the negative space.

35
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:15,720
So they go around and do art installations like this.

36
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:18,960
All the things that go up there, they said there's a life cycle, at first it starts with

37
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:25,400
the sexual organs, then it starts with cuss words, then it was bush, you know, slanders,

38
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:30,400
and then people actually got to art, but there was always a life cycle of presentations.

39
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,600
So I went home and I was having dinner with my wife and was telling her about this and

40
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:37,080
we were like, well, wait a second, if we know that this technology exists where you can

41
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:41,440
use your eyes to control things, why don't we figure out a way for Tempt to control a

42
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:44,520
laser and he can do graph again, that would be awesome.

43
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:50,520
So that started the journey and about two years later, about a year later, after a bunch

44
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:54,920
of organization and a bunch of kind of moving things around, we'd accomplished a couple

45
00:02:54,920 --> 00:02:55,920
of things.

46
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:59,520
One, we battered down the doors of the insurance companies and we actually got Tempt a machine

47
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:06,800
that let him communicate, a Stephen Hawking machine, yeah, which was awesome and he is

48
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:10,320
seriously one of the funny, I call him Yoda because you talk to the guy, you get an email

49
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:14,360
from him and you're like, I'm just, I'm not worthy, this guy is so amazing.

50
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:19,240
The other thing we did is we flew seven programmers from all over the world, literally every corner

51
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:21,640
of the world into our house.

52
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:27,160
My wife and kids and I moved to our back garage and these hackers and programmers and conspiracy

53
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:30,080
theorists and anarchists took over our house.

54
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:34,220
A lot of our friends thought we were absolutely stupid to do that and that we were gonna come

55
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:37,960
back and all the pictures on the wall would be removed and graph on the wall.

56
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:44,240
But for over two weeks, we programmed, we went to the Venice boardwalk, my kids got

57
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:51,000
involved, my dog got involved and we created this.

58
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,200
This is called the iRider and you can see the description.

59
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:59,200
This is a cheap pair of sunglasses that we bought at the Venice Beach boardwalk.

60
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:03,760
Some copper wire and some stuff from Home Depot and Radio Shack.

61
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:09,220
We took a PS3 camera, hacked it open, mounted it to an LED light and now there's a device

62
00:04:09,220 --> 00:04:13,560
that is free, you build this yourself, we publish the code for free.

63
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:18,080
We download the software for free and now we've created a device that has absolutely

64
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:19,640
no limitation.

65
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:23,320
There's no insurance company that can say no.

66
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:25,440
There's no hospital that can say no.

67
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:28,960
Anybody who's paralyzed now has access to actually draw or communicate using only their

68
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:29,960
eyes.

69
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:44,640
Thank you.

70
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:45,640
Thank you guys very much.

71
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:49,060
That was awesome.

72
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:53,000
At the end of the two weeks, we went back to Temp's room.

73
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,200
I love this picture because this is someone else's room and that's his room so there's

74
00:04:56,200 --> 00:04:59,940
all this hustle and bustle going on for the big unveiling.

75
00:04:59,940 --> 00:05:05,540
After over a year of planning, two weeks of programming and carb fest and all night

76
00:05:05,540 --> 00:05:09,260
sessions, Tony drew again for the first time in seven years.

77
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:13,920
This is an amazing picture because this is his life support system and he's looking over

78
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:14,920
his life support system.

79
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:19,500
We kicked his bed so that he could see out and we set up a projector on a wall out in

80
00:05:19,500 --> 00:05:24,120
the parking lot outside of his hospital and he drew again for the first time in front

81
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:29,260
of his family and friends and it's just like you can only imagine what the feeling in the

82
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:30,260
parking lot was.

83
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:32,820
The funny thing is we had to break into the parking lot too so we totally felt like we

84
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:37,460
were legit in the whole graph scene too.

85
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:42,100
At the end of this, he sent us an email and this is what the email said.

86
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:45,500
That was the first time I've drawn anything for seven years.

87
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:49,840
I feel like I'd been held under water and someone finally reached down and pulled my

88
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:51,860
head up so I could breathe.

89
00:05:51,860 --> 00:05:58,120
Isn't that awesome?

90
00:05:58,120 --> 00:05:59,360
That's kind of our battle cry.

91
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:05,000
That's what keeps us going and keeps us developing and we've got such a long way to go with this.

92
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,760
This is an amazing device but it's the equivalent of an Etch A Sketch and someone who has that

93
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:12,560
kind of artistic potential deserves so much more so we're in the process of trying to

94
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:15,960
figure out how to make it better, faster, stronger.

95
00:06:15,960 --> 00:06:19,320
Since that time, we've had all kinds of acknowledgement.

96
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:20,320
We've won a bunch of awards.

97
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:21,320
Remember, it's free.

98
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:22,920
None of us are making any money on this thing.

99
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:26,400
It's all coming out of our own pockets so the awards were like, oh, this is fantastic.

100
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:30,040
Armstrong twittered about us and then in December, Time Magazine honored us as one of the top

101
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:35,720
50 inventions of 2010 which is really cool.

102
00:06:35,720 --> 00:06:39,560
The coolest thing about this and this is what's kind of completing the whole circle is that

103
00:06:39,560 --> 00:06:44,880
in April of this year at the Geffen Mocha in downtown Los Angeles, there's going to

104
00:06:44,880 --> 00:06:48,760
be an exhibition called Art of the Streets and Art of the Streets is going to have pretty

105
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:51,400
much the bad asses of the street art scene.

106
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:56,520
Dixie, Shepard Fairey, Kaws, all of these guys will be there.

107
00:06:56,520 --> 00:07:03,040
Temp's going to be in the show which is pretty awesome.

108
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:06,120
Basically this is my point.

109
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:10,240
If you see something that's not possible, make it possible.

110
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:11,800
Everything in this room wasn't possible.

111
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:17,120
This stage, this computer, this mic, the iWriter wasn't possible at one point.

112
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:18,200
Make it possible.

113
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:20,680
Everyone in this room ... I'm not a programmer.

114
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:24,440
I've never done anything with ocular recognition technology but I just recognized something

115
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:28,360
and associated myself with amazing people so that we could make something happen.

116
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:32,240
This is the question I want everyone to ask yourself every single day when you come up

117
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:34,720
with something that you feel that needs to be done.

118
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:36,680
If not now, then when?

119
00:07:36,680 --> 00:07:37,680
If not me, then who?

120
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:38,680
Thank you guys.

121
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:39,680
Thank you.

122
